**Analyzing App Theme**

I've been examining the newly added `app-theme.css` file within the `Astra_master` project. It appears to define shared application-level styling, primarily setting a background color and font family. This is loaded after Azia's bundled styles, which suggests a customization or overriding strategy. I'm noting the use of CSS variables for flexibility.


**Examining Style Overrides**

I've observed further implications of `app-theme.css`. The file now seems to selectively override styles for several UI elements, ensuring consistency with the overall application theme. This includes adjustments to font family, background colors, and the general presentation of card components and dashboard elements. I am also investigating the impact on accessibility.


**Refining Dashboard Layout**

I'm now focusing on specific CSS rules for dashboard card layouts. I'm noting the use of `flex` properties to manage child element sizing and the impact on elements such as buttons in the card header. I'm also examining the grid layout of the `.card-body-top` section, and specifically, the use of `grid-template-columns` and spacing for data presentation. I'm paying close attention to the adjustments made for `.card-dashboard-one`, `.card-dashboard-two`, and `.card-dashboard-three` styles. My aim is to better understand responsive behavior.


**Investigating Card Style Adjustments**

I'm deep diving into specific style adaptations for various dashboard card types, specifically `.card-dashboard-three` and `.card-dashboard-four`. The changes include positioning, display, and size adjustments for charts and content within these elements. I'm also now analyzing the styling of the `.az-list-item`, `.az-traffic-detail-item`, and `.card-dashboard-five` components to understand their flexbox and layout strategies. Finally, the responsive changes added for smaller screens are showing how these elements render on mobile devices.


**Comparing Template Implementations**

I'm now comparing the `template/index.html` files of `Astra_master` and `Gemini_master` projects. I've noticed the `azia.css` and `app-theme.css` linkages present in `Astra_master` are consistent across its nine HTML files. I can confirm `app-theme.css` introduces a new background, and fixes the overlapping layout issues in the dashboard.


**Improving Layout Clarity**

I've been working on specific layout refinements to address overlapping and wrapping issues. I've adjusted the positioning of the `.card-header` in `.card-dashboard-three` to prevent overlap with the `.card-body` and chart, setting the `.card-header` to `position: static; max-width: none;` and height `auto`. I have also fixed flex-wrap and gap issues across several components like `.card-dashboard-two .card-header h6`, `.card-dashboard-three .card-header h6`, `.az-dashboard-one-title`, and `.az-dashboard-nav`, ensuring more predictable content flow. Also, I've resolved wrapping and alignment issues with `.az-list-item`, `.az-traffic-detail-item`, `.card-dashboard-five`, and `.az-footer`. I am now focusing on implementing the new font family: "Segoe UI", "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif across the application.


**Confirming CSS Override Logic**

I've just confirmed the critical point: `app-theme.css` is loaded *after* `azia.css`. This means its rules *will* override `azia.css`'s. I'm now examining how this override strategy affects font-family declarations, specifically targeting any hardcoded font families within `azia.css` and the SCSS files. I have identified `body`, `.az-dashboard-date .date > div:first-child`, and `.card-dashboard-one .card-body-top h2` as elements to examine.


**Identifying Font Consistency**

I'm now cataloging font-family declarations across various components to ensure consistency. I've noted specific usages of 'Archivo' and 'Poppins' within header and title elements, including `.card-dashboard-two .card-header h6`, `.card-dashboard-three .card-header h6`, `.card-dashboard-four`, `.card-dashboard-five h4`, `.az-header .az-logo`, `.az-signin-header h1`, and `.az-signup-header h2`. Also, I am checking the inheritance properties used by Bootstrap for headings and form elements. I'm focusing on how `app-theme.css` targets a broader range of elements to apply a consistent font style. I need to determine if `.az-dashboard-title` has a specific style in `azia.css`.


**Examining Override Strategy**

I'm now formulating the CSS rule to universally apply the `--app-font` variable, excluding icon fonts. The goal is font consistency across headings, body text, inputs, and other text elements without disrupting icon font rendering. I've drafted a selector that specifically targets relevant HTML elements and class selectors, but I'm being careful to exclude icon-related classes. I will also check the git diff for other changes.

```css
body,
input, button, select, textarea,
h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6,
.az-logo, .az-content-breadcrumb, .az-dashboard-date .date > div:first-child,
.card h2, .card h4, .card h6, .az-signin-header h1, .az-signup-header h2 {
  font-family: var(--app-font);
}
```


**Verifying Redirect Mechanism**

I'm now confirming the redirect from the root `index.html` to the `template/index.html` file in both the `Astra_master` and `Gemini_master` projects. I've verified the presence of the JavaScript redirect in the root `index.html` file of the `Gemini_master` project. I've rechecked all nine template files in `Astra_master` to ensure consistent linking of `azia.css` and `app-theme.css`, always in that order. I am now certain of the link to the core template.


**Refining Style Implementation**

I'm now implementing the `app-theme.css` file with the style overrides, and I'm ensuring all nine HTML template files in `Astra_master` consistently link it after `azia.css`. This ensures that font styles and other CSS variables are applied across the entire application. The focus is now on confirming that the font overrides are applied correctly and consistently throughout the various templates, while maintaining the overall layout integrity. I am now double-checking the selectors I implemented.

```css
/* Shared application theme. Loaded after the bundled Azia styles. */
:root {
  --app-background: #edf2f8;
  --app-font: "Segoe UI", "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif;
}

body {
  background: var(--app-background);
  font-family: var(--app-font);
  line-height: 1.5;
  color: #334155;
}

input, button, select, textarea,
h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6,
.az-logo, .az-content-breadcrumb, .az-dashboard-date .date > div:first-child,
.card h2, .card h4, .card h6, .az-signin-header h1, .az-signup-header h2 {
  font-family: var(--app-font);
}

.az-content, .az-signin-wrapper, .az-signup-wrapper {
  background: var(--app-background);
}
```


**Implementing Theme Variables**

I'm now focusing on implementing the `--app-background` variable to elements, including `.az-content`, `.az-signin-wrapper`, and `.az-signup-wrapper`. I'm applying the `--app-font` to headings and body text, and examining Bootstrap's styling for input and button elements. I have also fixed the card-header style.

\n\n


**Considering Chart.js Integration**

I'm now integrating the font overrides with Chart.js, specifically checking if `Chart.defaults.global.defaultFontFamily` needs adjustment. I'm focusing on the `Chart.bundle.min.js` inclusion in `template/index.html` and how it might interact with the new font.

